WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:05.710
Hey, Thoughty2 here.

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:12.340
The film Groundhog day, released back in 1998,
is set in the town of Woodstock - no, not

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:17.790
that one - and tells the story of Bill Murray’s
character Phil Connors, who repeatedly relives

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:20.380
February 2nd.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:26.210
Hey, Thoughty2 here.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:31.301
The film Groundhog day, released back in 1998,
is set in the town of Woodstock - no, not

00:00:31.301 --> 00:00:37.250
that one - and tells the story of Bill Murray’s
character Phil Connors, who repeatedly relives

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:38.390
February 2nd.

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:42.020
Wait, did I do that bit already?

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:47.860
Chances are that, like me - and good old Bill
Murray - you’ve had that strange feeling

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:53.370
where you feel like you’ve already experienced
a situation before, even though you know you

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:54.370
haven’t.

00:00:54.370 --> 00:01:00.590
That sensation, of course, is déjà vu, and
between 60 and 80% of us will experience it

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:02.200
at some point in our lives.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:06.410
But what exactly is déjà vu, and what causes
it?

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:12.720
Well, according to professor of psychological
science James Lampinen, déjà vu is “a

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:17.700
strong sense of global familiarity that occurs
in a seemingly novel situation.”

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:22.280
It’s that little nagging voice in your head
saying “I’ve been here before” when

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:27.110
you know that’s not the case, unless you’re
eating cheese straight out of the fridge at

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:30.049
2am – we both know it’s not your first
time.

00:01:30.049 --> 00:01:36.400
The phenomenon was first named by a French
parapsychologist Emile Boirac in 1876.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:43.120
The name itself is fairly self-explanatory—déjà
vu means “already seen” in French.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:48.590
But why was it a parapsychologist - someone
who studies psychic phenomena - who was so

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:50.350
interested in it?

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:54.630
Well at the time, déjà vu was considered
so strange it was thought to be something

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:59.200
supernatural, much like clairvoyance or mediumship.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:04.690
For many years, it was considered an area
unworthy of study, most often explained away

00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:10.640
as a side effect of reincarnation or possibly
even alien abduction.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.019
Never been in this room before that somehow
feels so familiar?

00:02:15.019 --> 00:02:18.450
Well then, you must have been here in a past
life, obviously!

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:26.079
Either that or aliens vigorously probed you
on this very spot before wiping your brain.

00:02:26.079 --> 00:02:27.099
But over the years, the scientific community
has begun to take déjà vu more seriously

00:02:27.099 --> 00:02:28.099
- though the same can’t be said for the
general public - a 1991 Gallup poll of attitudes

00:02:28.099 --> 00:02:29.099
towards déjà vu saw it placed alongside
astrology, paranormal activity, and ghosts—you

00:02:29.099 --> 00:02:30.099
know, the holy trinity of ridiculous superstitious
beliefs.

00:02:30.099 --> 00:02:33.579
The name déjà vu itself has become a catchall
term for unexpected familiarity, but there

00:02:33.579 --> 00:02:37.489
are actually several different varieties of
the phenomenon to remember.

00:02:37.489 --> 00:02:43.849
Déjà entendu is the sense you’ve already
heard something that’s in fact new to you

00:02:43.849 --> 00:02:50.129
– not to be confused with Deja Nintendo,
which is when you suddenly remember you’ve

00:02:50.129 --> 00:02:54.120
been playing Pokemon for six days straight
and need to get a life.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:03:00.299
There’s also, déjà pensé, the feeling
you’ve had a particular thought before,

00:03:00.299 --> 00:03:02.150
when in reality you haven’t.

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:10.260
Déjà gouté relates to food, and déjà
voulu, weirdly enough, is the sense you’ve

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:14.590
desired something - or someone - before, when
you haven’t.

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:19.939
It’s important to note that déjà vu is
completely different to precognition, which

00:03:19.939 --> 00:03:23.449
is where people have the feeling they know
what’s about to happen.

00:03:23.449 --> 00:03:28.560
That’s not déjà vu as much as it is claiming
to be psychic and mental.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:33.029
To be on the safe side, psychologists have
actually tested this out.

00:03:33.029 --> 00:03:37.879
Back in the 1950s, when scientists had free
rein to attach whatever they felt like to

00:03:37.879 --> 00:03:44.799
people’s brains, direct stimulation of the
frontal cortex, which induced déjà vu, was

00:03:44.799 --> 00:03:51.359
found to also produce the feeling of premonition—but
without granting any actual power to predict

00:03:51.359 --> 00:03:54.829
things or see the future in any way.

00:03:54.829 --> 00:04:00.920
And more recent, less brain-proby experiments
have found exactly the same thing.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:04.709
These days, we separate déjà vu into two
distinct types.

00:04:04.709 --> 00:04:10.609
The first, what we’d consider “regular”
déjà vu, is seemingly random, and the second

00:04:10.609 --> 00:04:15.339
is associated with neurological conditions
like temporal lobe epilepsy.

00:04:15.339 --> 00:04:21.269
This latter form is usually accompanied by
other symptoms and is termed pathological

00:04:21.269 --> 00:04:22.370
déjà vu.

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:28.640
It’s caused by disease or injury of some
kind, particularly damage to or malfunction

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:33.200
of the frontal lobe, the part of the brain
that handles memory.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:37.871
Pathological déjà vu is easier to study,
since it occurs far more frequently in the

00:04:37.871 --> 00:04:42.870
individuals it affects, and researchers have
been able to identify the areas of the brain

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:44.950
where the feeling originates.

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:49.730
But pathological déjà vu is generally considered
to be a little bit different from your normal

00:04:49.730 --> 00:04:51.070
everyday déjà vu.

00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:55.680
For instance, those suffering from this condition
may actually think they’ve been in a given

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:02.290
situation before for sustained periods of
time, rather than merely experiencing a fleeting

00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:03.290
feeling.

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:09.450
A British man, Pat Long, suffered from one
of the worst cases of déjà vu on record.

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:14.350
In his mid-thirties, Pat was diagnosed with
a brain tumour the size of a lemon after suffering

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:16.310
epileptic seizures.

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:21.540
To most of us, déjà vu is nothing more than
a strange moment, a kind of ‘huh, that’s

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:24.550
weird’, that lasts no more than a second
or two.

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:28.590
Pat’s déjà vu was something else entirely.

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:35.790
In fact, it wasn’t really déjà vu at all,
but déjà vecu, meaning ‘already lived’

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:40.960
- we’re building up quite the nice little
list of French vocab here, aren’t we - anyway,

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:46.800
as the name suggests, Pat’s déjà vecu
meant he would believe he had lived whole

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.500
sequences of events before.

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:54.800
The feeling was so strong and would last so
long that for weeks on end he would be almost

00:05:54.800 --> 00:06:01.850
entirely unable to differentiate between past
experiences and the present, with memories,

00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:08.840
hallucinations, and the products of his own
imagination all interchangeable to him.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:14.341
Epilepsy sufferers often experience bouts
of déjà vu during seizures, as well as the

00:06:14.341 --> 00:06:21.240
experience described as the opposite of déjà
vu - jamais vu - Meaning “never seen”.

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:28.400
Jamais vu is when a situation that should
be familiar instead feels foreign for some

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:32.810
reason, as if you’re experiencing it for
the first time.

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:39.510
While not quite so famous as it’s cousin
déjà vu, jamais vu is easier to induce—repeat

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:47.140
any word aloud enough times in a short space
of time and it will slowly start to lose its

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:59.690
meaning meaning meaning meaning meaning meaning
meaning meaning meaning meaning “meaning”…meaning?

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:04.590
Inducing déjà vu is far more difficult,
which means studying the sensation can be

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:09.500
tricky - that’s part of the reason we still
don’t know exactly how it works or what

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:10.660
causes it.

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:15.320
For most of us, it’s not a daily occurrence,
so getting data on a significant scale in

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:17.710
a lab setting is difficult.

00:07:17.710 --> 00:07:20.460
Difficult, but not impossible.

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:26.780
As part of a 2006 study, scientists at Leeds
Memory Group used hypnosis in an attempt to

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:29.270
induce déjà vu.

00:07:29.270 --> 00:07:35.020
They theorised the sensation was caused by
a kind of malfunction in memory processing.

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:39.840
When the human brain is presented with a new
scene, it carries out an internal check to

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:43.540
see whether elements of the scene have been
observed before.

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:50.270
If they have, a different part of the brain
identifies the scene as familiar.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:56.020
The researchers hypothesised that déjà vu
occurs when the second part of this process

00:07:56.020 --> 00:08:03.120
is triggered without the first, and they were
able to successfully use hypnosis to artificially

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:08.310
trigger the malfunction simply by telling
hypnotised participants that the next time

00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:14.200
they saw a particular word, they would know
it was familiar but not know where they’d

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:15.990
last seen it.

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:18.040
Does that explain the mystery then?

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:20.030
Well, not necessarily.

00:08:20.030 --> 00:08:24.810
Because other researchers attempting to recreate
déjà vu have come to their own, sometimes

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:28.100
very different conclusions.

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:33.780
According to a 2014 study conducted at the
University of St Andrews in Scotland, déjà

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:40.069
vu may actually be a healthy and important
neurological failsafe that performs a conflict

00:08:40.069 --> 00:08:44.440
resolution function to prevent false memories
from forming.

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:50.050
As part of the experiment, researchers used
word association techniques to convince participants

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:57.509
they’d been shown certain words they hadn’t,
effectively forming simple ‘false memories’.

00:08:57.509 --> 00:09:03.110
When later quizzed on these memories, around
two thirds of participants reported experiencing

00:09:03.110 --> 00:09:04.550
déjà vu.

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:10.350
Interestingly, MRI scans of their brains showed
activity not in the hippocampus, which is

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:15.970
responsible for memory, but in the frontal
cortex, which is responsible for decision

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:17.170
making.

00:09:17.170 --> 00:09:21.949
The researchers theorised that déjà vu was
experienced as the brain attempted to resolve

00:09:21.949 --> 00:09:28.040
the conflict between what the participants
had actually seen, and what they thought they’d

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:29.040
seen.

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:33.949
As interesting as these ingenious lab-based
explorations of déjà vu are, it’s also

00:09:33.949 --> 00:09:39.589
possible there’s a much simpler explanation
to all this - that déjà vu is simply triggered

00:09:39.589 --> 00:09:42.370
by a ‘forgotten’ memory.

00:09:42.370 --> 00:09:47.050
After all, it’s well established that less
important and infrequently accessed memories

00:09:47.050 --> 00:09:52.360
are eventually forgotten, or at least suppressed
to some extent.

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:57.170
Déjà vu may just be the feeling we get when
something we’re experiencing is similar

00:09:57.170 --> 00:10:02.889
to one of these discarded memories that’s
gone but not-quite-forgotten.

00:10:02.889 --> 00:10:07.500
Frequent travellers are also thought to experience
déjà vu more regularly than others, which

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:11.879
perhaps supports this theory - the more places
you’ve been and the more different things

00:10:11.879 --> 00:10:16.699
you’ve seen and done, the more likely it
is that a seemingly new experience might feel

00:10:16.699 --> 00:10:22.649
familiar to you, because it’s similar to
something you no longer quite remember - consciously,

00:10:22.649 --> 00:10:23.649
at least.

00:10:23.649 --> 00:10:28.899
We can’t talk about Déjà vu without giving
a quick shout out to Sigmund Freud, who believed

00:10:28.899 --> 00:10:34.920
the phenomenon was caused by repressed desires
or old traumatic memories, because, well,

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:39.089
that guy linked everything to repressed desires
and trauma.

00:10:39.089 --> 00:10:44.779
He also had a suspicion that regular suffers
of déjà vu were most likely fixated with

00:10:44.779 --> 00:10:49.920
their mother’s genitals, but let’s face
it, Freud wasn’t happy until he’d gotten

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:55.670
at least one set of genitals into each of
his theories, so we can probably take that

00:10:55.670 --> 00:10:57.540
with a pinch of salt.

00:10:57.540 --> 00:11:04.779
Saying that, there was some method to his
genital-based madness on this occasion - Freud

00:11:04.779 --> 00:11:10.949
reasoned that your mother’s ‘secret garden’
is the only place in the world every single

00:11:10.949 --> 00:11:16.029
one of us has been just once before (I would
hope).

00:11:16.029 --> 00:11:22.230
Whether or not people born by caesarian section
experience déjà vu is, so far as I’m aware,

00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:23.230
unknown.

00:11:23.230 --> 00:11:26.589
There’s a thesis subject for you budding
psychologists out there.

00:11:26.589 --> 00:11:30.370
Make sure you give me a mention in the acknowledgements.

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:34.870
Another older explanation, albeit one that
remains a valid theory today, is known as

00:11:34.870 --> 00:11:36.860
dual processing.

00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:41.829
It suggests our experiences are handled in
both hemispheres of the brain, and any delay

00:11:41.829 --> 00:11:47.850
in processing from one side may cause that
eerie feeling of déjà vu.

00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:53.700
This was tested by Robert Efron in 1963, and
he found that the temporal lobe of the brain’s

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:57.819
left hemisphere is where incoming information
is sorted.

00:11:57.819 --> 00:12:03.310
But he also found that the temporal lobe receives
this information both directly and after a

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:05.879
short detour through the right hemisphere
of the brain.

00:12:05.879 --> 00:12:11.850
If, for any reason, the information traveling
through the right hemisphere is delayed at

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:19.129
all, it could be assigned the wrong timestamp,
giving a feeling of familiarity we assume

00:12:19.129 --> 00:12:23.870
is from long ago, but is in reality from mere
moments before.

00:12:23.870 --> 00:12:29.160
This idea that déjà vu may actually be a
sense of familiarity from extremely recent

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:35.439
events rather than past ones underpins another
possible cause of the phenomenon - divided

00:12:35.439 --> 00:12:36.480
attention.

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:41.000
The idea here is that when your attention
is split as you experience a new situation

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:46.079
- for example if you’re talking to a friend
as you enter a pub for the first time - it

00:12:46.079 --> 00:12:50.879
may be that you take in your surroundings
subliminally without really paying attention

00:12:50.879 --> 00:12:52.129
to them.

00:12:52.129 --> 00:12:56.970
In essence, you’re subconsciously observing
the situation faster than you consciously

00:12:56.970 --> 00:12:58.399
process it.

00:12:58.399 --> 00:13:03.970
The gap between the completion times of these
two processes isn’t noticeable enough that

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:07.249
you’d suddenly pause like a robot without
a command…

00:13:07.249 --> 00:13:13.880
…but it is enough to think you’d experienced
the event before, since, as in the dual processing

00:13:13.880 --> 00:13:19.809
theory, you kind of already have, fractions
of a second ago.

00:13:19.809 --> 00:13:25.240
To test this, Marsh and Brown showed groups
of students photographs of various locations

00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:27.499
and asked them if they were familiar.

00:13:27.499 --> 00:13:32.579
Unbeknownst to the students, some of the images
had been flashed onto the screen just before

00:13:32.579 --> 00:13:38.139
for a very short time, only 10 to 20 milliseconds,
which was long enough for the students’

00:13:38.139 --> 00:13:43.899
brains to pick up on the images, but not long
enough to consciously notice them.

00:13:43.899 --> 00:13:49.449
As expected, the locations that had flashed
up, like those creepy transition shots in

00:13:49.449 --> 00:13:56.220
The Ring, had wormed their way into the students’
subconscious and were recognised as familiar,

00:13:56.220 --> 00:14:01.209
even though the students had never been to
any of them.

00:14:01.209 --> 00:14:07.579
Whichever theory you like the sound of, the
truth is they’re all still just that - theories.

00:14:07.579 --> 00:14:12.860
Despite science’s best efforts, we still
don’t know for sure what causes déjà vu.

00:14:12.860 --> 00:14:18.190
One of the latest studies, published in 2019,
found that people who experienced déjà vu

00:14:18.190 --> 00:14:22.989
more frequently used different parts of their
brain to retrieve memories than people who

00:14:22.989 --> 00:14:25.380
rarely or never do..

00:14:25.380 --> 00:14:30.740
In particular, frequent déjà vu-ers—I’m
well aware I’ve completely massacred the

00:14:30.740 --> 00:14:36.720
French language by this point—showed less
activity in their hippocampi, the so-called

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:42.480
librarians of the brain that help consolidate
memories, suggesting that people who don’t

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:46.529
get déjà vu may simply be those with better
memories.

00:14:46.529 --> 00:14:52.350
Equally, if you consider the fact that déjà
vu is most prevalent in people between 15

00:14:52.350 --> 00:14:58.019
and 25 years old, perhaps it’s just a sign
that we’re not senile yet and can still

00:14:58.019 --> 00:15:04.230
tell real memories from fake ones—a comforting
thought for anyone in that age bracket, and

00:15:04.230 --> 00:15:07.189
a sobering one for all of us over the threshold.

00:15:07.189 --> 00:15:11.279
Of course, I couldn’t go this whole video
without a quick nod to the more out-there

00:15:11.279 --> 00:15:17.420
explanations, like that from the Denzel Washington
thriller appropriately titled Déjà vu, where

00:15:17.420 --> 00:15:23.420
it’s the result of FBI-sponsored time travel,
and particularly the one made famous by Keanu

00:15:23.420 --> 00:15:25.269
Reeves wearing all black.

00:15:25.269 --> 00:15:30.509
No, I’m not talking about John Wick, or
his adverts for Saint Laurent menswear, or

00:15:30.509 --> 00:15:35.499
even his red carpet appearance at the Toy
Story 4 premiere—seriously Keanu, don’t

00:15:35.499 --> 00:15:36.920
get typecast or anything.

00:15:36.920 --> 00:15:40.600
No, I’m talking about his rise to superstardom
in The Matrix.

00:15:40.600 --> 00:15:44.839
Do I need to do a spoiler alert for a 21-year-old
film?

00:15:44.839 --> 00:15:50.439
Anyway, as you doubtless remember, in the
film, Neo sees the same cat cross a doorway

00:15:50.439 --> 00:15:57.569
twice and nonchalantly comments, “oh, déjà
vu” as if seeing the world literally repeat

00:15:57.569 --> 00:16:00.899
itself before your very eyes is no big deal.

00:16:00.899 --> 00:16:07.309
His team very much disagree, jumping into
action as Trinity explains that déjà vu

00:16:07.309 --> 00:16:11.560
is usually a glitch in the matrix that happens
when they change something.

00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:16.579
And hey, if you want to take déjà vu as
evidence we’re all just motionless bodies

00:16:16.579 --> 00:16:21.999
plugged into the machine, I’m not gonna
stop you.

00:16:21.999 --> 00:16:30.389
Thanks for watching.

